  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in South Dakota total $478M in FY2026 to date. Award activity so far includes 2,652 awards with an average award size of $180K, and obligations fell 40.6% year over year. Recent fiscal years were larger: FY21 was $624M, FY22 was $1.2B, FY23 was $1B, FY24 was $1.3B, and FY25 was $804M. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state with $195M in obligations, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $101M and the Department of the Interior with $86.3M. Top vendors so far are KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC with $64.9M and FEDERAL CONTRACTING INC with $40.4M. Industry obligations are concentrated in construction and IT-related NAICS, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $94.2M and Computer Facilities Management Services at $65.2M. All figures reported are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why are FY2026 obligations lower than recent years?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 40.6% year over year, reflecting a sharp decline in FY2026 to date compared with the prior fiscal year total of $804M in FY25. FY26 at $478M is also below the peaks seen in FY22 and FY24, which were $1.2B and $1.3B respectively. The current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date, so the year-to-date figure represents obligations recorded so far."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal spending in South Dakota?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounts for $195M of FY2026 to date obligations, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $101M and the Department of the Interior with $86.3M. The largest contractors are KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC at $64.9M and FEDERAL CONTRACTING INC at $40.4M. Industry concentration is highest in construction and IT services, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $94.2M and Computer Facilities Management Services at $65.2M."
      }
    ]
  },
